Milestone 4 – Final Development Report

Project: Five Faces of Evil

1. Project Overview

Five Faces of Evil is a C++ text-based murder mystery game where players investigate the murder of an agent connected to five suspicious individuals. Players can review suspects, examine evidence, and interrogate individuals to uncover information surrounding the case.

The goal of the final development milestone was to improve the application’s stability, refine existing functionality, strengthen input validation, and prepare the project for its final release.

2. Features Completed

The application currently includes:

* A main menu with options to begin an investigation or exit the game.
* An investigation menu for reviewing suspects and examining evidence.
* Five unique suspects, each with an occupation, alibi, and individual dialogue.
* An interrogation system with three questioning options for each suspect.
* Six pieces of evidence containing descriptions and investigative details.
* Navigation options that allow players to move between menus and return to previous sections.
* Console text formatting to give the game a distinct visual presentation.

3. Refactoring and Code Improvements

Several improvements were made to strengthen the organization and readability of the application.

* Refined the Suspect and Evidence constructors using member initializer lists.
* Continued using separate classes to keep suspect and evidence responsibilities organized.
* Maintained reusable menu input validation through the GetMenuChoice() function.
* Improved input handling to recognize end-of-input conditions.
* Preserved the use of references when passing suspect objects into interrogation functions.
* Maintained consistent menu structures and navigation throughout the application.

These changes helped improve code organization, reduce repetition, and make the application easier to maintain.

4. Bugs Resolved and Testing

The final development review included rebuilding and testing the application.

The following areas were verified:

* Successful compilation in Visual Studio.
* Successful application startup and execution.
* Main menu navigation.
* Investigation menu navigation.
* Suspect selection and interrogation.
* Evidence selection and examination.
* Invalid text input handling.
* Returning between menus without unexpected behavior.

Menu input validation was also improved to account for end-of-input conditions, helping prevent menus from repeatedly displaying when input is no longer available.

The application successfully built and completed runtime testing without any issues during the final verification.

5. Usability Improvements

The application provides clear menu options and consistent navigation so players can move through the investigation without becoming lost.

Additional improvements include:

* Clear prompts when requesting player input.
* Error messages when invalid text or menu selections are entered.
* Individual dialogue for each suspect to make interrogations more engaging.
* Descriptive evidence reports that provide context for each discovery.
* Consistent return options throughout the investigation system.

These improvements make the experience easier to navigate and provide more meaningful interaction with the game’s suspects and evidence.

6. Future Development

Although the main investigation experience is functional, there are additional features that could expand the game in future development.

Potential improvements include:

* Implementing a final accusation system that allows players to identify the murderer.
* Connecting collected evidence to individual suspects.
* Implementing persistent case notes to track examined evidence.
* Expanding investigative choices and dialogue.
* Introducing additional replayability through randomized details and outcomes.

These ideas represent opportunities to build on the existing foundation rather than unfinished features being presented as completed work.

7. Final Reflection

Building Five Faces of Evil has provided an opportunity to apply C++ programming concepts through a project developed from the ground up. Throughout development, I focused on organizing classes, separating responsibilities, debugging issues, and testing functionality.

One of the biggest takeaways was learning how important it is to build, test, and refine a project throughout development rather than waiting until the end. Using GitHub Issues and the Project board also helped organize tasks and keep development moving forward.

The final application represents the progress made throughout the course and provides a foundation for future improvements.
